WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.960
In the fall of 2008, the economy
careened into unknown territory.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.550
Forty-one percent told Gallup pollsters the
events then made them feel personally afraid.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:17.020
Many felt the financial system could collapse.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:21.289
Over the next several years, workers’ anxieties
about their jobs rose.

00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:26.640
In Gallup’s data, between 2008 and 2009,
the number of Americans who feared losing

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.769
their job or having their pay cut doubled.

00:00:28.769 --> 00:00:29.869
Many told pollsters they knew someone who
had lost a job.

00:00:29.869 --> 00:00:32.860
Confidence in banks and financial institutions
plummeted.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:37.590
Americans blamed banks and Wall Street for
greed; they also blamed regulators for a lack

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.600
of financial regulation which they believed
contributed to the crisis.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.930
What has changed in the last decade?

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:48.600
Americans’ confidence about their jobs and
wages has returned to pre-crash levels, and

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.230
they feel very positive about the job market
right now.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.460
Confidence in banks and Wall Street has inched
up.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:59.040
But only small number of Americans believe
our financial system is more secure than it

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.580
was then.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:06.820
Around a third gave that response to Pew in
2013 and 2015 while around 60 percent said

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:07.820
it was not.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.720
How do you remember the financial crisis? Let us know in your comments?

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.020
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:21.280
and be sure to like and subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

